Description: Name the programs Debian installs under another name
 Debian Policy 10.1: "Two different packages must not install programs with
 different functionality but with the same filenames ... If this case happens,
 one of the programs must be renamed." So `fd` is fd-find's `fdfind`
 (fdclone has /usr/bin/fd) and `bat` is bat's `batcat` (bacula-console-qt has
 /usr/sbin/bat), and command-not-found, which reads only the Contents index,
 offered fdclone for `fd` and bacula-console-qt for `bat`.
 .
 The data directory's renamed-commands file lists those programs: the command,
 the package, the name Debian installs it as, and "first" or "after". For a
 listed command, advise() names the package with the name to run, before the
 packages and snaps that have the command under its own name ("first") or
 after them ("after"); those stay listed as before. A package the database
 does not have is not offered; one whose program is installed is said to be
 installed. A command with no row is advised exactly as before. A table that
 cannot be read (missing, unreadable, a broken row) is said in one line, with
 the way to restore it, and the command is advised as without the table.
 The upstream tests now read the source tree's own data directory.
 .
 Ubuntu's priority.txt (Michael Vogt, 2009, LP: #318442) ordered packages that
 have the command itself and left with the sqlite database in 2018; neither
 project has a renamed-commands table (searched 2026-11-02: Launchpad and
 salsa main, merge requests, Debian's and Launchpad's bugs).
 The three new messages ship in pl, pt_BR, fr, de, ja, ko, zh_CN and zh_TW.
